1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:05,467
Shapes have holes, and mathematicians keep a careful list of them.

2
00:00:05,467 --> 00:00:11,100
Each loop that cannot be shrunk is one entry on that list.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:17,700
Some entries come from smaller shapes drawn inside, shapes cut out by equations.

4
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:24,500
Do all the entries that look like they should, really come from such shapes?

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,900
That is the Hodge conjecture, a Millennium Prize problem.

6
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:36,800
In 2026, OpenAI claimed to prove it for one important family of shapes.

7
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:51,500
Algebraic geometry studies shapes cut out by polynomial equations. A circle is the simplest example.

8
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,866
Such shapes are called varieties.

9
00:00:56,866 --> 00:01:03,633
Allow the coordinates to be complex numbers, and even simple equations give rich shapes.

10
00:01:03,633 --> 00:01:09,733
The complex solutions of this cubic equation form a doughnut.

11
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,800
Topology records the holes of a shape.

12
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:19,966
On a doughnut, a loop around the hole and a loop around the tube cannot be shrunk to a point.

13
00:01:19,966 --> 00:01:25,066
Each one gives a cohomology class.

14
00:01:25,066 --> 00:01:34,166
Higher-dimensional shapes have holes of every dimension, and so classes in every degree.

15
00:01:34,166 --> 00:01:39,533
Now draw a smaller shape inside, itself cut out by equations.

16
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:47,266
It is called an algebraic cycle, and it has a class too: its shadow.

17
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:57,333
So: classes record holes, and shapes cut out by equations cast shadows among them.

18
00:02:01,566 --> 00:02:09,066
For complex shapes, every class splits into pieces labelled by two numbers, p and q.

19
00:02:09,066 --> 00:02:15,066
Roughly, they count two kinds of directions.

20
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:21,766
Here are the sizes of those pieces for a three-dimensional example, arranged in a diamond.

21
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:31,800
Shadows of cycles always land in the balanced middle column, where p equals q, and they have rational coefficients.

22
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:38,800
A class that passes both tests is called a Hodge class.

23
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:48,433
The Hodge conjecture says the converse: every Hodge class is the shadow of some rational combination of algebraic cycles.

24
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:53,600
Hodge posed it in 1950.

25
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:59,300
In degree two it is known, by a theorem of Lefschetz from 1924.

26
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:05,266
Multiplying those classes together reaches higher degrees too.

27
00:03:05,266 --> 00:03:17,233
The hard part is any Hodge class that is not built that way: an exotic class. For those, someone has to find the cycle.

28
00:03:17,233 --> 00:03:26,333
So every Hodge class should be a shadow, and the exotic ones are the hard part.

29
00:03:30,566 --> 00:03:36,400
Take the complex plane and a lattice, a grid of points.

30
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:41,300
Glue each point to every point that differs from it by a lattice step.

31
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:48,800
Opposite edges of the shaded cell meet, and the plane becomes a doughnut.

32
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:54,333
Do the same in more complex dimensions, and you get an abelian variety:

33
00:03:54,333 --> 00:04:02,600
a many-holed, higher-dimensional doughnut that can still be cut out by equations.

34
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,033
Most lattices have no special symmetry.

35
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:14,966
But the square lattice is carried to itself by a quarter turn, which is multiplication by i.

36
00:04:14,966 --> 00:04:26,833
When an abelian variety has the largest possible set of such symmetries, it is said to have complex multiplication, or to be CM.

37
00:04:26,833 --> 00:04:33,900
CM varieties are rare, only countably many, yet spread densely among all the others.

38
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:42,233
Their symmetries are governed by number fields, which makes them central in number theory.

39
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:47,000
They are also where the hardest test cases of the Hodge conjecture live:

40
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:55,566
exotic classes, called Weil classes, that are not built from products of simple ones.

41
00:04:55,566 --> 00:05:06,633
So: CM abelian varieties are doughnuts with extra symmetry, and they carry exotic Hodge classes.

42
00:05:10,866 --> 00:05:13,333
Earlier work set the stage.

43
00:05:13,333 --> 00:05:23,700
In 1982 Deligne proved that Hodge classes on abelian varieties have a strong arithmetic property shared by shadows of cycles.

44
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:33,500
In 2025, Eyal Markman proved the conjecture for all abelian varieties of dimension four.

45
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:40,900
Here is the claim. On every abelian variety with complex multiplication, of any dimension,

46
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:48,166
every Hodge class is the shadow of a rational combination of algebraic cycles.

47
00:05:48,166 --> 00:05:53,233
That includes all the exotic ones.

48
00:05:53,233 --> 00:06:01,400
So the claim is the Hodge conjecture for every CM abelian variety.

49
00:06:05,633 --> 00:06:10,367
The proof has five stages. First, a reduction.

50
00:06:10,367 --> 00:06:18,433
Each basic CM piece is described by a list of signs, one for each pair of directions.

51
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:25,967
The paper shows that every Hodge class on a CM variety can be assembled from one kind of building block:

52
00:06:25,967 --> 00:06:33,567
a class on four pieces whose signs balance, the first two adding up to the last two.

53
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:40,100
So it is enough to show that every balanced block is a shadow.

54
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:49,167
Second, a surface does the work. If some surface maps to the four pieces, and a certain integral over it is not zero,

55
00:06:49,167 --> 00:06:57,300
then the image of that surface is an algebraic cycle whose shadow is the block.

56
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:06,300
Third, the surface is built from theta functions on a quotient of the complex ball, a classical object in number theory.

57
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:11,933
The paper shows the integral is not zero.

58
00:07:11,933 --> 00:07:15,133
Fourth, arithmetic.

59
00:07:15,133 --> 00:07:27,133
Using Hecke operators and the Frobenius map modulo primes, it shows the theta forms come from exactly the right CM pieces.

60
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:36,000
Fifth, everything is assembled on one surface and brought back to rational coefficients.

61
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:48,467
So: balanced blocks, a surface with a nonzero integral, built from theta functions, and checked by arithmetic.

62
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:57,300
If it holds, older theorems of James Milne give more.

63
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:07,100
The Tate conjecture, the cousin of the Hodge conjecture over finite fields, would follow for every abelian variety.

64
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:15,700
So would one of Grothendieck's standard conjectures for abelian varieties, in every characteristic.

65
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:24,134
And several companion papers use this result to prove the Hodge conjecture for further families.

66
00:08:24,134 --> 00:08:34,367
One family settled, and with it the Tate conjecture for abelian varieties over finite fields.

67
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:46,067
On the twenty-sixth of October, OpenAI added a computer-checked proof of the main theorem in Lean.

68
00:08:46,067 --> 00:08:54,234
We have not re-run it, and it does not cover the consequences for the Tate conjecture.

69
00:08:54,234 --> 00:09:03,167
Three other Hodge papers from the same release were withdrawn in October, after a sign error in one of them.

70
00:09:03,167 --> 00:09:13,600
We checked the source: this paper does not cite any of them, and its one revision only updates a citation.

71
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:22,100
As of the fourth of November, we found no public assessment of the written proof by an expert.

72
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:31,967
So: a computer-checked main theorem, and a written proof that experts have yet to assess.

73
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:39,234
It does not solve the Millennium problem.

74
00:09:39,234 --> 00:09:49,067
The conjecture is about every smooth projective variety, and CM abelian varieties are one special family among them.

75
00:09:49,067 --> 00:09:55,900
General abelian varieties, K3 surfaces, and most other shapes are not covered.

76
00:09:55,900 --> 00:10:05,967
The proof's tools, theta functions, Hecke operators and Frobenius, are special to this family.

77
00:10:05,967 --> 00:10:17,300
But for the shapes with the most symmetry, and some of the strangest Hodge classes, every class may now be a shadow.
